WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.901
On the Calls tab in Omni IC, choose New Call.

00:00:03.081 --> 00:00:06.729
This walkthrough uses a fictional coordination
exercise.

00:00:06.909 --> 00:00:10.728
Create a call only when your department has
authorized you to do so.

00:00:11.048 --> 00:00:15.272
Enter a short name and use Nature to explain what the
team needs to know.

00:00:15.452 --> 00:00:17.607
Select the priority and call type.

00:00:17.787 --> 00:00:23.440
Here we use Routine and Investigation so the exercise
is easy to distinguish in the list.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.048
In Call Location, enter the verified coordinates for
the exercise.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:34.927
A clear location matters whether you are coordinating
a search, an industrial drill, or a community
standby.

00:00:35.247 --> 00:00:39.364
Open Select Recipients and check the audience before
creating the call.

00:00:39.544 --> 00:00:43.192
The only selected person here is the signed-in
training user.

00:00:43.372 --> 00:00:45.377
The Done button shows one selection.

00:00:45.697 --> 00:00:48.215
Back on the form, check Dispatch To again.

00:00:48.395 --> 00:00:51.787
Confirm the intended recipient before choosing
Create.

00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:55.209
If the audience shown is wrong, change it before you
submit.

00:00:55.529 --> 00:00:57.108
Find the call in the list.

00:00:57.288 --> 00:01:01.235
Here the server returned a call number and the call
is marked Assigned to me.

00:01:01.415 --> 00:01:06.897
A queued call has not been dispatched yet, so check
for the server result before relying on it.

00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:10.524
Open the saved call and check the name, number, and
map.

00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:15.568
The detail page keeps that context with the call's
notes, images, and files.

00:01:15.888 --> 00:01:18.576
Open Call actions, then choose Edit Call.

00:01:18.756 --> 00:01:24.068
Change the existing call so the revised instruction
stays with the same incident number.

00:01:24.388 --> 00:01:26.905
In Nature, update the briefing instruction.

00:01:27.085 --> 00:01:31.373
The example tells the crew that the briefing moved to
the marked staging point.

00:01:31.693 --> 00:01:34.211
Scroll to the end of the form and choose Save.

00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:38.977
Keep the existing recipient scope unless the exercise
calls for a change.

00:01:39.297 --> 00:01:43.159
Reopen the same call and confirm the revised briefing
appears.

00:01:43.339 --> 00:01:47.712
Then open Files to add a supporting document that
belongs with this call.

00:01:48.032 --> 00:01:52.832
Choose Add File and select the fictional briefing
from the device's file picker.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:56.916
Use a file that your department permits you to attach
to the incident.

00:01:57.236 --> 00:02:00.073
Wait for the briefing file to appear in Call Files.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:04.179
Its visible name lets you confirm you selected the
right document.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:06.589
Close and reopen the Files panel.

00:02:06.769 --> 00:02:09.329
The briefing is still listed on the same call.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:16.485
This is useful during a handoff because the next
operator can find the latest briefing alongside the
incident details.
